    {
      "section": "Workflow memory",
      "text": "Lets a workflow keep what worked and follow it on the next run, with the aim of speeding up complex workflows."
    },
    {
      "section": "Workflow memory",
      "text": "- **Use it**: turn it on for a step, or drag **Memory** from **Basics** in the step library onto the canvas as one shared by the whole workflow; pick an existing entry or create a new one when you add it.\n- **Content**: an entry is a skill folder, and the \"Quick path\" at its top is the cheapest way to do the task next time. Each step reports what it learned in its reply, and when the workflow ends the model folds that in.\n- **Manage it**: view and version entries on the **Memory** page in the workflow hub; choose whether to include them when you export.\n- **Limits**: instances running at the same time that change the same memory are not locked, so the later write overwrites the earlier one; re-exporting a Skill over an installed copy overwrites the memory it has built up."
    },
    {
      "section": "Workflow memory",
      "text": "Memory is only a configuration shortcut. For anything more elaborate, build it yourself with a resource plus prompts.\n\n**We are still checking whether it helps, and we'd like your feedback.** In early trials that repeated one workflow, we saw no steady speedup; the \"Quick path\" was added afterwards, and whether it helps needs more real workflows to tell."
    },
    {
      "section": "How it works",
      "text": "1. **Design**: arrange steps on the canvas, write the prompt each executor receives, and describe the flow with branches and loops.\n2. **Compile**: the plugin turns the workflow into one plan prompt that states the order of work, loop conditions, and each step's inputs and outputs.\n3. **Run**: the model decides how to carry out the plan (working directly, dispatching subagents or forming a team) and reports progress back to the canvas."
    },
    {
      "section": "How it works",
      "text": "The plugin ships no execution engine and needs no extra service; scheduling and judgement stay with the model in your current session."
